# Appended to ~/pa-compose/Caddyfile on the VPS. The live Caddy belongs to the
# agentchat stack; this stack only borrows it. See setup.md §5a.
#
# Order matters, and each step exists because skipping it has a known failure:
#
# 1. DNS FIRST. `marketing` A → 159.69.208.172 and AAAA → 2a01:4f8:1c1e:c964::1
#    at Porkbun. Until then the name falls through the `*` wildcard to Porkbun's
#    parking page. Add this block before DNS resolves and Caddy's ACME challenge
#    fails and backs off with growing delays — which presents as a TLS error.
#
# 2. Caddy must be on the `edge` network or `mra:8000` does not resolve:
#      docker network connect edge caddy     (live, no restart)
#    and persisted in ~/pa-compose/docker-compose.yaml so a recreate keeps it.
#
# 3. Edit the Caddyfile IN PLACE (`cat new > Caddyfile`), never with a tool that
#    writes a new file and renames it. It is a single-file bind mount: the
#    container holds the original inode, so a replaced file is invisible to it
#    and `caddy reload` silently re-reads the old config.
#
# 4. Apply with zero downtime to chat.vanis.ai:
#      docker exec caddy caddy reload --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
#
# Hostname is literal rather than `{$MRA_DOMAIN}`: an env var is read at
# container start, so it would force a Caddy recreate — and a blip on chat — for
# a value that never changes.
#
# `flush_interval -1` is not optional. Without it Caddy buffers the SSE event
# stream and a live run arrives in one lump at the end, which looks exactly like
# a hung agent.
marketing.vanis.ai {
	encode zstd gzip

	reverse_proxy mra:8000 {
		flush_interval -1
	}
}
